    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "This behavior is due to how pandas handles DataFrames when you assign them to a new variable. When you do `df_subset = df[file1, file2]`, it creates a view of the original DataFrame (`df`) instead of a copy. \n\nBy default, when you assign this view to a new variable (`df_subset`), the reference to the original DataFrame is broken. Any changes you make to `df_subset` won't affect the original DataFrame because they're not actually modifying the underlying data, but rather the reference to it. To avoid this issue, it's generally safer to create a copy of the slice like you did earlier (+`.copy()`), or to use the `inplace=True` argument when assigning or using operations on a DataFrame.",
